sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-27> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "A Turing machine with just 2 states and 5 colors, far simpler than the 7-state 4-color machine that had long been the simplest known, is universal because it emulates rule 110 by sweeping back and forth and updating all cells by one rule 110 step per sweep.";
    dc:type "mathematical";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A 2-state 5-color Turing machine is universal via rule 110";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 11, “The Notion of Computation”; section “Universality in Turing Machines and Other Systems” (from p. 706), p. 707. Scope: small universal Turing machines"
}
